{"batchcomplete":"","continue":{"gsroffset":10,"continue":"gsroffset||"},"query":{"pages":{"191040814":{"pageid":191040814,"ns":6,"title":"File:Ceremonial Pen Used By President Lyndon Johnson To Sign The Voting Rights Act.jpg","index":5,"imagerepository":"local","imageinfo":[{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d8/Ceremonial_Pen_Used_By_President_Lyndon_Johnson_To_Sign_The_Voting_Rights_Act.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","descriptionurl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ceremonial_Pen_Used_By_President_Lyndon_Johnson_To_Sign_The_Voting_Rights_Act.jpg","descriptionshorturl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=191040814","extmetadata":{"DateTime":{"value":"2026-05-03 01:40:43","source":"mediawiki-metadata","hidden":""},"ObjectName":{"value":"Ceremonial Pen Used By President Lyndon Johnson To Sign The Voting Rights Act","source":"mediawiki-metadata"},"CommonsMetadataExtension":{"value":1.2,"source":"extension","hidden":""},"Categories":{"value":"CC-Zero|Fountain pens|Voting Rights Act of 1965|Creative Commons CC0 1.0 Universal Public Domain Dedication missing SDC copyright license|Images from the Smithsonian Institution","source":"commons-categories","hidden":""},"Assessments":{"value":"","source":"commons-categories","hidden":""},"ImageDescription":{"value":"When a U.S. President signs a bill, a select number of people receive pens used for the occasion. This ceremonial pen was given to journalist Ethel L. Payne (1911-1991) in recognition for her civil rights activism. The fountain pen\u2019s metal nib connects to a black, plastic feed followed by a tapered, translucent barrel, also plastic. A black and gold frame holds the pen above a summary of the bill, Senate 1564. President Lyndon B. Johnson used this ceremonial pen to sign the Voting Rights Act of 1965 into law on August 6, 1965. The previous year, President Johnson presented Payne with a ceremonial pen at the signing of 